# login: auth account password session
#
# A CIDER CONFIGURATION, not the Apple one, and the difference is deliberate.
#
# Apple ships a login policy built on pam_opendirectory, which asks a directory service about the
# account. There is no directory service in this container. Without a policy of its own, openpam
# falls back to /etc/pam.d/other, which is pam_deny for every facility, so login printed
# "Login incorrect" for a name that getpwnam resolves perfectly well. That is what iTerm2 hit:
# it opens a session with login -fp $USER and never got a shell.
#
# There is nothing here for authentication to protect. The container has one account, root, whose
# home is the prefix; cider shell already runs anything in it without a password; and the guest as
# a whole runs with the privileges of the host user who started it. A policy that pretended to
# authenticate would be theatre, and a policy that denies is what we had.
#
# So: succeed for the container account and say so plainly. pam_rootok covers the normal case,
# which is the session that login itself starts as root, and the permit lines below it keep the
# remaining facilities from falling through to the deny in other.
auth       sufficient     pam_rootok.so
auth       required       pam_permit.so
account    required       pam_permit.so
password   required       pam_permit.so
# NO pam_launchd.so HERE, and optional would not have saved it. openpam reports
# "no pam_launchd.so found" and then pam_start fails outright with a system error, so naming a
# module that cannot be loaded breaks the whole policy however it is marked. login then exits 1
# with nothing on stderr, because it reports that failure to syslog.
session    required       pam_permit.so
